WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:08.340
In the first film, TGF-β -beta bridged two kinases and sent a Smad complex into the

2
00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:14.920
nucleus. Yet that same complex halts many healthy epithelial cells and can help late

3
00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:20.580
tumours spread. How can one signal give opposite answers, and what holds it back?

4
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.040
Watch how a Smad complex meets DNA.

5
00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:31.360
Its DNA-binding domain reads a tiny four-base tag, such as GTCT, and grips it

6
00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:38.000
weakly. Each base is one of four letters, so a four-base tag appears by chance about

7
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.180
once every 256 bases.

8
00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:44.420
That is millions of times across a human genome.

9
00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:48.180
A Smad complex alone cannot pick a gene.

10
00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:54.230
Each cell type makes its own partner transcription factors and keeps its own

11
00:00:54.230 --> 00:01:01.510
chromatin open. A Smad complex usually locks onto DNA firmly only beside a partner

12
00:01:01.810 --> 00:01:03.190
reading a nearby sequence.

13
00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:07.810
The Smad carries one message: TGF-β beta is here.

14
00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:10.870
The partners decide what that means.

15
00:01:11.883 --> 00:01:18.903
In many epithelial cells, partners turn on p15 and p21 to stop division, and shut

16
00:01:18.903 --> 00:01:24.223
off Myc. In fibroblasts, different partners switch on collagen genes.

17
00:01:25.523 --> 00:01:31.003
Early in cancer, TGF-β -beta restrains tumours because cells obey the stop order.

18
00:01:32.343 --> 00:01:37.863
Later, in cells that escape arrest, TGF-β -beta can push them to move, and it quiets

19
00:01:37.863 --> 00:01:39.263
nearby immune cells.

20
00:01:40.317 --> 00:01:41.637
Pause and predict.

21
00:01:43.297 --> 00:01:48.917
Suppose a tissue's matrix holds plenty of TGF-β -beta protein, yet nearby cells show

22
00:01:48.917 --> 00:01:53.637
no Smad signal. What could keep that protein away from its receptors?

23
00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:02.920
A signal this powerful must stay off most of the time.

24
00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:05.920
Three layered brakes hold it back.

25
00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:10.120
First, TGF-β -beta is made with a wrapper piece.

26
00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:16.100
The wrapper is cut free but stays clamped around it, tethered to the extracellular

27
00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:22.440
matrix. Active dimer is freed locally, for example when an integrin pulls or a

28
00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:23.440
protease cuts.

29
00:02:24.283 --> 00:02:28.523
Second, the active signal makes its own inhibitor inside the cell.

30
00:02:30.103 --> 00:02:33.983
Active Smads switch on the gene for inhibitory Smad7.

31
00:02:33.983 --> 00:02:40.723
Smad7 returns to cap the active type I receptor, blocking incoming Smads in negative

32
00:02:40.723 --> 00:02:46.223
feedback. Smad6 plays a similar role, preferring the BMP branch.

33
00:02:47.283 --> 00:02:52.943
Third, Smad7 recruits ubiquitin ligases called Smurfs to the active receptor.

34
00:02:54.303 --> 00:02:58.643
Smurfs tag the receptor, and some Smads, with ubiquitin for destruction.

35
00:03:00.263 --> 00:03:03.203
Together, these layered brakes keep signaling local and brief.

36
00:03:04.143 --> 00:03:09.583
Breaking a key part, like Smad4 or the GS safety catch, can drive human disease.

37
00:03:10.767 --> 00:03:14.567
So one short relay depends on both context and control.

38
00:03:16.027 --> 00:03:21.887
Inside the nucleus, partner factors and open chromatin decide which genes respond to

39
00:03:21.887 --> 00:03:28.307
TGF-β. beta. Outside and at the membrane, layered brakes decide where and how long

40
00:03:28.307 --> 00:03:29.147
the signal lasts.
